<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uyghurs</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Uyghur</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Uyghurs are a Turkic people who founded a powerful khaganate on the Mongolian steppe in the mid-eighth century after overthrowing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-turkic-khaganate">Second Turkic Khaganate</a> in alliance with others. The Uyghur Khaganate became a major ally of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a> before its collapse in 840. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Gokturk Empire</a> treat the Uyghurs as successors to Göktürk power in the steppe.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uyghurs.jpg" alt="Uyghurs" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uyghur_man_in_Kashgar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Uyghurs are a Turkic people of Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> who speak Uyghur, a Karluk language. Their Turkic origin comes mainly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karluks">Karluks</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate</a>, which migrated into the region, and their ancestry also draws on the Tocharians and East Iranian peoples who were indigenous there. Scholars hold that the history of the people is contested. Uyghur historians have given the people a very long history, while the official Chinese view dates the ethnic group from the collapse of the Uyghur Khaganate in 840. Many Western scholars do not see the modern Uyghurs as direct descendants of the khaganate, but as descendants of several peoples, one of them the ancient Uyghurs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Uyghurs of the khaganate belonged to a Turkic confederation called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiele">Tiele</a>, who lived south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-baikal">Lake Baikal</a> and around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yenisei-river">Yenisei River</a>. The Uyghur Khaganate lasted from 744 to 840 and was governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordu-baliq">Ordu-Baliq</a>, one of the largest ancient cities built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. In 840, after a famine and civil war, it was overrun by the Yenisei Kirghiz, and most of its tribal groups dispersed. Some Uyghurs moved to Jimsar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> and founded kingdoms, among them the Ganzhou Kingdom (870 to 1036), which was absorbed by the Western Xia. They mixed with the Tocharians of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> and adopted oasis agriculture.</p><p>The ancient Uyghurs held many local beliefs, including shamanism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tengrism">Tengrism</a>, and adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a> as the state religion of the khaganate, possibly in 762 or 763; later, in Qocho, many were Buddhists. Inhabitants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> were gradually converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> after the 10th century, and by the 16th century most identified as Muslims. The region came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> in the 18th century after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungar-qing-wars">Dzungar–Qing Wars</a> and became the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>. Until 1921 the people of the Tarim Basin used terms such as Kashgarlik and Yarkandlik; in that period Turkic intellectuals meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> adopted the name Uyghur.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Uyghur literature in the Kara-Khanid period is regarded by modern Uyghurs as an important part of their tradition. It includes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-balasaguni">Yusuf Khass Hajib</a>'s Kutadgu Bilig, written in 1069–70, and Mahmud al-Kashgari's dictionary of Turkic dialects of 1072. In the 20th century two short-lived East Turkestan republics were proclaimed, in 1933 and in 1944. Today the Uyghurs are the titular nationality of the Xinjiang Uyghur Autonomous Region in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, one of the country's 55 officially recognised ethnic minorities, and communities live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uyghurs” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Kaidu's Ögedeid domains and the Chagatai khan Duwa (r. 1282–1307), whom Kaidu had installed, then waged sustained war against the Yuan dynasty of Kublai Khan and his successors in Central Asia, raiding the <b>Uyghur</b> lands and Mongolia and keeping pressure on the Yuan frontier for nearly three decades.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ashina clan's confederation model was inherited by the <b>Uyghur</b> and Khazar khaganates, and the broader Turkic political tradition eventually flowed into the Seljuk and Ottoman empires.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gokturk-empire">Gokturk Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>For centuries they had alternated between submission to and raids on Tang China, the Turks and the <b>Uyghurs</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Yassa, traditionally attributed to Genghis Khan, provided a framework for military discipline and social order, and the adoption of the <b>Uyghur</b> script gave the Mongols their first written administration.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Victor Spinei holds that the first certain reference to them is an 8th-century <b>Uyghur</b> envoy's report, surviving in Tibetan, of fighting between the Be-ča-nag and the Hor near the Syr Darya.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pechenegs">Pechenegs · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Xi Jinping control has tightened: from about 2017 large numbers of <b>Uyghurs</b> and other Muslims in Xinjiang were held in detention camps, and a National Security Law imposed on Hong Kong in 2020 ended most organised opposition there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
